The dataset contains student information including age, gender, course, marks, and attendance. Exploratory Data Analysis showed that the dataset contains no missing values, making it suitable for analysis without additional cleaning. The statistical summary indicates that student marks are generally high, with an average around 87, while attendance is consistently above 90%. Histograms reveal that marks are distributed fairly evenly without extreme outliers. The correlation heatmap suggests a positive relationship between marks and attendance, indicating that students with higher attendance tend to achieve better academic performance. The course count chart shows that AIML and CSE have the largest number of students, while ISE has fewer records. Overall, the dataset is clean and well-structured. If this were a real-world dataset, additional checks such as duplicate detection, outlier analysis, and feature engineering would further improve its quality before training a machine learning model. This analysis provides useful insights into student performance and demonstrates the importance of data exploration before building predictive models.
